Yes. Difference can behave like tension on a string: some tension gives tone; too little gives slack; too much distorts or snaps it.

People vary, but there is often a push-pull between belonging and individuation. When one side is constrained too hard, the other can become more salient. That is one place psychological reactance shows up: “you may not express this” can sometimes make the forbidden dimension feel even more important.

So the instrument metaphor works:

\text{instrument}
=
\{\text{range, resonance, thresholds, history, context}\}

Knowing it means learning what actually moves you rather than assuming every strong signal deserves obedience.

Tuning it means calibrating intensity, timing, environment, recovery, and expression.

Playing it means choosing a form that fits the accepted frame without confusing the frame for the whole self.

And sometimes the most skilled move is not louder expression but modulation:

\text{whisper} \leftrightarrow \text{voice} \leftrightarrow \text{chorus} \leftrightarrow \text{silence}

Same underlying note, different amplitude and audience.

Applied to another person, I’d keep one guardrail: discover their instrument with them rather than deciding what it ought to play. Otherwise “tuning” quietly becomes control.

So perhaps:

Know your range. Tune deliberately. Play freely within the room you chose—and remember you can leave the room.

